#include <string.h>
#include <stdio.h>

char config_buf[64];

void parse_config_line(char *line, int len) {
    memcpy(config_buf, line, len);
    config_buf[len] = '\0';
}

int main() {
    char line[] = "key=value";
    parse_config_line(line, strlen(line));
    printf("%s\n", config_buf);
    return 0;
}

The bug is that when len is the string length, writing config_buf[len] writes one past the end of the string data, but the buffer is only 64 bytes. If len equals 64, that's an overrun. Even for shorter strings, the copy `memcpy(config_buf, line, len)` copies exactly len bytes, then writes the null terminator at index len, which is the next byte. If len is 63, that's fine (indices 0-63 total 64 bytes), but if len is 64, it writes at index 64 which is out of bounds. The fix is to ensure we copy at most 63 bytes and null-terminate. Change the function to:

```c
void parse_config_line(char *line, int len) {
    int copy_len = len < 63 ? len : 63;
    memcpy(config_buf, line, copy_len);
    config_buf[copy_len] = '\0';
}
```

