I'm implementing a singly linked list in Rust for a systems programming assignment. The code is in `src/linked_list.rs` and I'm hitting borrow checker errors when trying to implement `push_back` and `remove`. Here's the relevant part of my code:

```rust
pub struct Node<T> {
    pub val: T,
    pub next: Option<Box<Node<T>>>,
}

pub struct LinkedList<T> {
    head: Option<Box<Node<T>>>,
}

impl<T> LinkedList<T> {
    pub fn push_back(&mut self, val: T) {
        let mut current = &mut self.head;
        while let Some(node) = current {
            current = &mut node.next;
        }
        *current = Some(Box::new(Node { val, next: None }));
    }

    pub fn remove(&mut self, index: usize) -> Option<T> {
        if index == 0 {
            return self.head.take().map(|n| n.val);
        }
        let mut current = &mut self.head;
        for _ in 0..index {
            if let Some(node) = current {
                current = &mut node.next;
            } else {
                return None;
            }
        }
        current.take().map(|n| n.val)
    }
}
```

The error I get is:
```
error[E0499]: cannot borrow `*current` as mutable more than once at a time
  --> src/linked_list.rs:15:17
   |
15 |         while let Some(node) = current {
   |                 ^^^^^^^^^^^ mutable borrow starts here in previous iteration of loop
16 |             current = &mut node.next;
   |                 ----- mutable borrow ends here
```

Also there's a similar error in `remove`. I need to understand the ownership issue and fix the implementation. Can you help me debug this and provide a corrected version that compiles? Also, explain why the original fails and how the fix works.

